T39.01|Bank analysis connects loan quality, funding, margins, operating costs and capital. Deposits are financing obligations rather than ordinary product sales; loans generate interest but also credit exposure. Standard enterprise-value multiples can be awkward because debt and cash are integral to bank operations.~~Evaluate earnings together with provisions, capital and liquidity. Rapid loan growth can raise current income while weakening future credit quality.|Income engine: Interest spread and fee services.;;Asset quality: Defaults, provisions and recoveries.;;Resilience: Funding, liquidity and capital.|Average earning assets are ₹1,000 lakh and net interest income ₹30.~~Illustrative NIM is 3%.;;If credit losses rise by ₹15, assess the net earnings effect.;;Review deposits and capital alongside the margin.~~A healthy-looking spread does not establish sound total profitability.~~Reporting definitions vary.|Analyse credit cohorts and concentration.;;Review deposit stability.;;Reconcile earnings with capital and liquidity.
T39.02|Nonbank finance and housing lenders often depend on wholesale borrowing or other permitted funding rather than a broad bank-deposit franchise. Their economics link asset yields, funding costs, credit losses and operating expenses. Long-tenor loans can create rollover exposure when funding matures earlier.~~Compare borrower segments and collateral carefully. Secured lending can still lose money if collateral is illiquid or recovery is delayed.|Asset yield: Income on financed exposures.;;Funding profile: Source, tenor and repricing of liabilities.;;Credit economics: Defaults and net recovery costs.|A lender earns ₹12 lakh on ₹100 loans, pays ₹7 funding cost and ₹3 credit/operating costs.~~Pretax contribution is ₹2.;;A 2-point funding-cost rise eliminates it if nothing reprices.;;Inspect liability maturity and asset resets.~~A narrow spread can be sensitive to funding conditions.~~Loan timing and accounting require fuller analysis.|Map asset-liability maturities.;;Analyse credit and recovery.;;Stress wholesale funding and spreads.
T39.03|Insurance-sector analysis differs between life, health and general businesses. Underwriting profitability, reserve adequacy, distribution costs, investment returns and solvency all matter. Premium growth can consume capital or introduce losses rather than immediately increasing value.~~Use product-appropriate measures: combined ratios fit certain nonlife contexts, while life analysis often needs actuarial value and persistency measures.|Underwriting: Pricing and claims economics.;;Reserves/capital: Obligations and loss-bearing resources.;;Investment/persistency: Asset returns and continuing policies.|An insurer writes ₹100 lakh earned premiums with claims ₹70 and expenses ₹25.~~Underwriting surplus is ₹5 on those definitions.;;A ₹10 reserve strengthening turns that component negative.;;Assess investment income and capital separately.~~Premium growth without reserve analysis is incomplete.~~Actual measurement follows the reporting framework.|Choose product-specific metrics.;;Study reserve development.;;Review distribution, capital and investment matching.
T39.04|Asset managers often earn fees related to managed assets; brokerages earn transaction, distribution or other service revenue. Market levels, client flows, pricing and activity affect earnings differently. Client assets are generally not company revenue or freely usable corporate cash.~~Analyse recurring fee quality, retention and operating leverage. High trading activity can be temporary and may bring conduct or operational risks.|Asset management: Fees on managed mandates.;;Brokerage: Trading and related client services.;;Distribution: Compensation from product placement.|An asset manager has ₹1,000 lakh AUM at assumed 1% revenue rate and ₹7 costs.~~Revenue is ₹10.;;Profit is ₹3.;;A 20% AUM decline reduces revenue to ₹8 and profit to ₹1 if costs stay fixed.~~Market-linked revenue can amplify profit volatility.~~Flows and fee mixes also change outcomes.|Separate client assets from company funds.;;Analyse flows and fee rates.;;Stress market levels and fixed costs.
T39.05|Exchanges and market-infrastructure firms earn from trading, clearing, listings, data or custody services under their models. Network effects can support scale, while concentration, technology reliability and regulation create important risks. High margins require continued investment and resilience.~~Distinguish cyclical volume revenue from recurring services. Market share is valuable only if the activity remains relevant and permitted.|Trading services: Revenue linked to transactions or volumes.;;Data/listing services: Access and issuer-related fees.;;Infrastructure: Clearing, custody or settlement economics.|An exchange earns ₹6 lakh volume-linked and ₹4 recurring revenue, with ₹7 costs.~~Profit is ₹3.;;If volume revenue falls 50%, total revenue becomes ₹7.;;Profit becomes zero if costs are unchanged.~~Revenue mix determines cyclical sensitivity.~~Fees, competition and regulation require separate assessment.|Analyse revenue mix.;;Review uptime and investment.;;Stress volume, regulation and competition.
T39.06|Software, SaaS and IT services have different revenue and delivery models. SaaS depends on subscriptions, retention and product costs; IT services often depend on staff utilisation, pricing and project execution. Capitalised development and customer acquisition can affect reported margins and cash conversion.~~Compare recurring revenue definitions carefully. Bookings, contracted value, revenue and cash receipts are distinct.|SaaS: Subscription product economics and retention.;;IT services: People, utilisation and project margins.;;Software licensing: Rights and maintenance under contracts.|A SaaS cohort starts with ₹100 lakh recurring revenue, loses ₹10 and expands ₹20 from existing clients.~~Ending cohort revenue is ₹110.;;Net revenue retention is 110%.;;New-customer revenue is excluded from that cohort calculation.~~Retention metrics explain growth quality.~~Profitability still needs acquisition and servicing costs.|Reconcile recurring metrics to accounts.;;Track cohorts and margins.;;Inspect capitalised costs and cash receipts.
T39.07|E-commerce and marketplace businesses must distinguish gross transaction value from revenue. Inventory-owning models bear product and working-capital risk; marketplaces may earn commissions while facilitating trades. Incentives, fulfilment, refunds and returns affect contribution economics.~~Customer growth is valuable only when repeat behaviour and unit economics support it. Accounting principal-versus-agent treatment depends on the actual arrangement.|Inventory model: Buys and sells goods.;;Marketplace model: Connects buyers and sellers.;;Fulfilment economics: Delivery, returns and servicing costs.|A marketplace records ₹200 lakh GMV at a 12% take rate, with ₹18 variable costs and incentives.~~Illustrative revenue is ₹24.;;Contribution is ₹6.;;Check recognised revenue and customer cohort costs separately.~~GMV growth can outpace economically useful income.~~Definitions must match contracts and reporting rules.|Separate gross and net measures.;;Track repeat cohorts and returns.;;Analyse contribution and working capital.
T39.08|Consumer-goods and retail analysis examines volumes, pricing, brand strength, distribution and inventory. Manufacturers may sell to distributors before the final consumer; retailers' cash and margins depend on store economics and stock turnover. Channel loading can make sales look stronger than end demand.~~Separate price growth from unit growth and investigate promotions, returns and receivables. Brand value needs evidence of durable willingness to pay.|Consumer brand: Pricing, distribution and repeat demand.;;Retail store: Sales density, costs and inventory.;;Channel economics: Sell-in versus sell-through.|Revenue rises from ₹100 to ₹110 lakh while units fall 5%.~~Implied average price/mix factor is 1.10 ÷ 0.95 ≈ 1.158.;;Price/mix rises about 15.8%.;;Review margins and end-consumer demand.~~Revenue growth can coexist with weaker volumes.~~Mix changes may explain part of the increase.|Decompose volume, price and mix.;;Study inventory and channels.;;Assess promotions and cash conversion.
T39.09|Automotive and EV businesses depend on units, product mix, capacity utilisation, dealer channels and development investment. EV economics also include batteries, technology, charging and changing policy incentives. Manufacturing scale can improve cost but excess capacity creates fixed-cost pressure.~~Compare sustainable margins after warranties, development and required capex. Technology transitions can impair existing assets or create new investment needs.|Vehicle economics: Units, pricing and contribution.;;Capacity economics: Fixed cost and utilisation.;;Technology transition: Batteries, software and legacy assets.|A plant has ₹20 lakh fixed cost and ₹50,000 contribution per vehicle.~~Operating break-even is 40 vehicles.;;At 60 vehicles, contribution is ₹30 lakh and operating profit ₹10.;;Stress volume and warranty cost.~~Utilisation materially affects profitability.~~The example omits financing, tax and investment costs.|Analyse unit contribution.;;Track inventory and dealer demand.;;Review transition capex and warranties.
T39.10|Manufacturing and capital-goods firms convert orders into production, revenue and cash. Backlog can indicate demand but may contain cancellable orders, low-margin work or long delivery dates. Capacity, input costs, working capital and maintenance investment shape returns.~~Customer advances can support cash temporarily while creating delivery obligations. Revenue recognition should follow actual contractual performance.|Order book: Contracted future work under stated definitions.;;Production economics: Utilisation, material and labour costs.;;Cash conversion: Advances, inventory and receivables.|A manufacturer has ₹100 lakh backlog, but ₹30 is cancellable and delivery needs ₹20 working capital.~~Separate firm and uncertain orders.;;Model production costs and cash dates.;;Avoid valuing all backlog as profit.~~Backlog is a demand indicator, not a cash asset of equal value.~~Margins and completion risk require assessment.|Check backlog quality.;;Analyse utilisation and costs.;;Model working capital and capex.
T39.11|Metals, mining and commodity producers are sensitive to realised prices, grade, recovery, volumes and production costs. Cyclical peak earnings can make valuation multiples appear deceptively low. Reserves, environmental obligations and sustaining capex affect long-run cash value.~~Use through-cycle scenarios rather than only today's price. Producer leverage includes both operating fixed costs and financial debt.|Price exposure: Realised commodity prices and hedges.;;Resource economics: Grade, volume and extraction cost.;;Cycle economics: Cash generation across price regimes.|A producer sells 100 units at ₹10 with unit cash cost ₹7 and fixed cost ₹100.~~Operating contribution after fixed cost is ₹200.;;At price ₹8, it falls to zero.;;Add sustaining capex and financing separately.~~Small price changes can sharply affect earnings.~~Reserve and recovery assumptions need technical evidence.|Use cycle-aware prices.;;Review resource and cost quality.;;Include closure and sustaining investment.
T39.12|Oil, gas and petrochemical economics differ across upstream production, refining and downstream conversion. Upstream earnings follow realised prices, output and costs; refining depends on product-input spreads; petrochemicals depend on feedstock and product cycles. A rise in crude price need not benefit every segment.~~Integrated businesses can offset some exposures but still face inventory, regulation and major investment risks.|Upstream: Produces hydrocarbons.;;Refining: Converts crude into products.;;Petrochemicals: Converts feedstocks into chemical products.|A refiner's product basket earns ₹120 per unit and feedstock costs ₹100, with processing cost ₹10.~~Contribution is ₹10.;;If feedstock rises to ₹115 without product repricing, contribution becomes −₹5.;;Assess inventory and lagged pricing effects.~~Input-price gains can harm a downstream business.~~Actual yields and product mix require detailed modelling.|Analyse segment economics separately.;;Track spreads and inventory.;;Review capex, reserves and transition risks.
T39.13|Power, utilities and renewables differ in resource exposure, dispatch, demand and tariff arrangements. Regulated assets may earn under an allowed framework; merchant assets depend more on market prices; contracted renewables depend on resource, availability and buyer payment. Stability is conditional on contracts and regulation.~~Long asset lives require maintenance, grid access and financing resilience. A low operating cost does not imply zero investment or curtailment risk.|Regulated utility: Revenue influenced by an approved framework.;;Contracted generation: Output sold under agreed terms.;;Merchant generation: Exposed to market prices.|A renewable plant expects 1 lakh units at ₹5 each, with ₹2 lakh operating costs.~~Revenue is ₹5 lakh.;;Operating surplus is ₹3.;;A 20% output reduction lowers surplus to ₹2 if costs stay fixed.~~Resource and availability affect cash despite contracted prices.~~Debt, taxes and maintenance further reduce distributable cash.|Review tariff and payment terms.;;Stress output and curtailment.;;Model maintenance and financing.
T39.14|Telecom and digital infrastructure depend on subscribers or tenants, pricing, utilisation and large capital commitments. Average revenue per user helps explain revenue but must be combined with churn, acquisition cost and network spending. Spectrum, leases and upgrade cycles create additional obligations.~~Growth in data usage does not guarantee matching revenue growth. Networks can require investment before pricing improves.|Telecom service: Subscriber and usage economics.;;Tower/data centre: Tenant, capacity and contract economics.;;Network investment: Spectrum, equipment and upgrades.|A telecom serves 100,000 users at ₹200 monthly ARPU.~~Monthly revenue is ₹2 crore.;;If users grow 10% but ARPU falls 10%, revenue becomes ₹1.98 crore.;;Assess margins and network costs separately.~~Usage or subscriber growth can coexist with flat revenue.~~Metric definitions and subscriber quality matter.|Decompose users and ARPU.;;Track churn and investment.;;Review leases, spectrum and debt.
T39.15|Pharmaceutical, biotech and healthcare businesses have different risk drivers. Drug developers face research, approval and patent uncertainty; hospitals depend on capacity, payer mix and service quality; established manufacturers face pricing and compliance. Pipeline value should reflect probabilities, timing and investment.~~A successful scientific result is not necessarily a commercial approval or profitable product. Regulatory conclusions require current primary evidence.|Drug development: Pipeline probabilities and funding.;;Pharmaceutical sales: Products, pricing and exclusivity.;;Healthcare services: Utilisation, payer and operating costs.|A project has illustrative 20% success probability, success value ₹100 lakh and remaining cost ₹15 paid regardless.~~Simple expected success value is ₹20.;;Net expected value is ₹5 before discounting.;;Stress probability, timing and further costs.~~Large upside must be probability-weighted.~~This is not a clinical or regulatory assessment.|Separate scientific and commercial stages.;;Use probability and cash timing.;;Review compliance, patents and payer exposure.
T39.16|Real-estate developers and construction contractors differ from rental-property owners. Developers manage land, approvals, inventory and sales timing; contractors earn from delivery and contract terms; owners earn rent. Advances and reported profit can diverge from freely available cash.~~Study project-level funding and completion obligations. Consolidated margins can conceal troubled individual projects.|Developer: Creates and sells property.;;Contractor: Delivers construction services.;;Property owner: Holds assets for recurring rent.|A developer collects ₹50 lakh advances but must spend ₹60 to finish the related homes.~~Advance cash is not all surplus.;;Remaining completion funding gap is ₹10 before other resources.;;Check restrictions and sales collections.~~Cash collected can coexist with unfunded obligations.~~Revenue recognition depends on applicable contracts and accounting.|Analyse each project.;;Track completion costs and restricted cash.;;Review inventory, debt and sales quality.
T39.17|Airlines, airports, ports and logistics have distinct demand and cost structures. Airlines face fares, load factors, fuel and fleet costs; airports and ports earn from traffic and commercial services; logistics depends on network utilisation and delivery economics. Fixed assets create operating leverage.~~Capacity growth should be compared with demand and unit economics. Leasing and maintenance obligations belong in cash analysis.|Airline: Passenger yield, load factor and flight costs.;;Airport/port: Traffic, tariffs and commercial revenue.;;Logistics: Throughput, network density and delivery cost.|A flight offers 100 seats, sells 80 at ₹5,000 and incurs ₹3.5 lakh operating cost.~~Revenue is ₹4 lakh.;;Surplus is ₹0.5 lakh.;;At 60 seats sold, revenue ₹3 lakh produces ₹0.5 lakh loss if costs stay fixed.~~Load factors strongly affect profitability.~~Fleet, lease and financing costs add further obligations.|Use sector-specific unit metrics.;;Stress fuel, traffic and pricing.;;Include leases and maintenance.
T39.18|Hospitality, travel and restaurants depend on occupancy or footfall, pricing, capacity and service costs. Hotels often use occupancy, average daily rate and revenue per available room; restaurants require location and same-store economics. Seasonality and discretionary demand can make comparisons misleading.~~Separate growth from new capacity and improvement at existing sites. Owner, operator and franchise models bear different capital risks.|Hotel economics: Occupancy, ADR and room capacity.;;Restaurant economics: Visits, ticket size and store contribution.;;Asset-light model: Fees rather than full property ownership.|A hotel has 100 rooms, 70% occupancy and ₹5,000 average daily rate.~~Daily room revenue is ₹3.5 lakh.;;RevPAR is ₹3,500.;;Assess operating costs and compare like-for-like seasons.~~RevPAR combines volume and price.~~It excludes ancillary revenue and does not equal profit.|Compare comparable periods.;;Separate ownership and operating models.;;Track fixed costs and renewal capex.
T39.19|Agriculture finance depends on yields, weather, prices, inputs, storage and seasonal cash timing. Biological production cycles and perishability distinguish it from many ordinary businesses. Commodity price strength can be offset by weak harvests or higher inputs.~~Assess crop, location and value-chain stage separately. Insurance and contracted sales can reduce defined risks while leaving basis and execution exposures.|Production: Land, inputs and crop yield.;;Processing/storage: Conversion, spoilage and inventory.;;Distribution: Logistics and market access.|A farm expects 100 units at ₹10, with total cost ₹700.~~Base surplus is ₹300.;;A 30% yield decline cuts revenue to ₹700 if price stays unchanged.;;Review debt and seasonal payment needs.~~Yield risk can eliminate profit despite stable prices.~~Weather and price dependence may interact.|Model seasonal cash flows.;;Stress yields and input costs.;;Assess storage, insurance and market access.
T39.20|Media, entertainment and sports monetise audiences through subscriptions, advertising, rights, tickets and sponsorships. Revenue concentration, content performance and intellectual-property rights shape risk. Large audiences do not automatically translate into profitable paying demand.~~Content and player investments may precede receipts by years. Contract duration and rights ownership determine who captures value.|Subscription/advertising: Monetises continuing audiences.;;Content/rights: Revenue from creation and licensing.;;Sports/events: Tickets, sponsorship and media agreements.|A production costs ₹20 lakh and earns ₹12 rights sales plus ₹10 other revenue, with ₹4 distribution costs.~~Revenue totals ₹22.;;Net result is −₹2.;;Evaluate future rights and additional costs separately.~~Headline revenue success can conceal a loss.~~Rights and accounting timing affect the analysis.|Verify rights ownership.;;Model content-level cash flows.;;Stress audience, advertiser and contract concentration.
T39.21|Education and professional services sell expertise, access or credentials, with quality and reputation central to demand. Economics depend on enrolment or utilisation, pricing, staff costs and delivery capacity. Advance fees can finance operations but carry service obligations.~~Evaluate outcomes and retention alongside revenue. Scaling a service may require additional skilled people, limiting apparent operating leverage.|Education: Enrolment, completion and capacity.;;Professional services: Billable utilisation and pricing.;;Digital delivery: Platform scale with content and support costs.|A consultancy has ten staff, each able to bill 1,000 hours at ₹1,000, with 70% utilisation.~~Revenue is ₹70 lakh.;;At 60% utilisation it is ₹60 lakh.;;Compare fixed staff costs and collections.~~Utilisation changes revenue without reducing staffing proportionately.~~Realisation rates and nonbillable work require further detail.|Track quality and retention.;;Analyse utilisation and staff capacity.;;Reconcile advance fees and receivables.
T39.22|Government enterprises and regulated businesses operate under policy objectives, ownership structures and pricing constraints that may differ from ordinary firms. Public ownership does not automatically guarantee every liability. Regulation can stabilise some revenues while limiting returns or imposing service obligations.~~Separate commercial economics from subsidies, guarantees and policy mandates. Verify support in enforceable documents rather than relying on assumptions.|Public enterprise: State ownership with a defined mandate.;;Regulated pricing: Revenue set or constrained by rules.;;Public support: Subsidy or guarantee under specific terms.|A service costs ₹100 lakh, customer revenue is ₹80 and budgeted subsidy ₹20.~~Base funding balances at ₹100.;;If subsidy is delayed, operating liquidity can still fail.;;Check legal commitment and payment timing.~~Policy support and usable cash are different.~~No blanket state guarantee is implied.|Read the mandate and regulation.;;Separate subsidies from commercial revenue.;;Verify guarantees and payment timing.
